*This Project is heavily inspired from [this video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ApdLgfgfh5o) by Ballpark Figures.*
All code used for this project is available on [Github]()


## Hangman Rules
Hangman is a two player game, where one player is the *Game master*, deciding on
a secret word (in my case a noun in german). The other player tries to guess this 
word by asking for individual letters. If the secret word contains that letter, the
gamemaster reveals all positions, where that letter is at. If it doesn't, the player looses a Life.
Figuratively, one body part is added to a stickfigure hanging from the gallows.
The guessing player knows the number of Letters in the secret word. 
In the following, I will assume the guessing player has perfect knowledge of the wordset and the choosing player randomly selects a word from it.

I settled on hangman with 6 lives (Head, Body and 4 limbs) and set umlauts (Ä, Ö, Ü) and the 'sharp S' (ß) as separate characters.

## Taumata­whakatangihanga­koauau­o­tamatea­turi­pukaka­piki­maunga­horo­nuku­pokai­whenua­ki­tana­tahu

<figure class="plain">
	<img src="/img/hangman/NounDistribution.png" alt="A distribution of noun lenghts in german. It's in form of a bell curve with center 10 and max 84" />
	<figcaption> The distribution of noun lengths in german</figcaption>
</figure>

Taumata­whakatangihanga­koauau­o­tamatea­turi­pukaka­piki­maunga­horo­nuku­pokai­whenua­ki­tana­tahu is the longest word in my [Wordlist](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/gambolputty/german-nouns/main/german_nouns/nouns.csv). It has $85$ letters and apparently is the name of a hill in new Zealand. Note that in german, you can infinitely chain nouns together. `German-Nouns` only selects words that were mentioned in german Wikipedia.
Since the guessing player knows the number of letters at the start of the game, we can train a different decision tree for every word length. 

Each different guess can have a maximum of $2^{n}$ different outcomes, where $n$
is the number of unknown letters in the word.

## The Frequency tactic
<figure class="plain">
	<img src="/img/hangman/letterfreq.png" alt="A distribution of letter frequencies german. It's in form of a bell curve with center 10 and max 84" />
	<figcaption> The distribution of letter uses in german</figcaption>
</figure>

The first real tactic that comes to mind is picking the most frequent letters in order. It is flawed, because it completely disregards existing information the guessing player has: Letter count of the word, Existing successful guesses and his knowledge about the german language.

This naive tactic yields a winrate of about $9.0\%$ with an average number of $13.89$ misses per word. We are already significantly better than just choosing letters in *alphabetical order*, where we get a winrate of $0.2\%$ with $10.73$ avg misses per word.

## The perfect Strategy
At every game step, we can perform a letter frequency analysis on the remaining
word options and select the most frequent letter and thus make the optimal
greedy choice. This of course assumes perfect knowledge of the choosing players
wordset. After running for about 10 minutes on 500 samples per wordlength, this achieved a $97.2\%$ winrate with $1.21$ average misses. We mainly fail on short, unconventional words. Some examples for len 3-8:
```
Len 3: 'gäa' in 8 guesses (6 wrong: eoinbd, 6 to finish)
Len 3: 'sou' in 8 guesses (6 wrong: aebndg, 7 to finish)
Len 3: 'rtl' in 6 guesses (6 wrong: aeouid, 7 to finish)
Len 3: 'ivf' in 7 guesses (6 wrong: aeouhl, 8 to finish)
Len 3: 'ria' in 8 guesses (6 wrong: eocdfm, 7 to finish)
Len 4: 'tipp' in 8 guesses (6 wrong: aeonkl, 7 to finish)
Len 4: 'crow' in 8 guesses (6 wrong: aelgbf, 6 to finish)
Len 4: 'smog' in 8 guesses (6 wrong: aelrnh, 6 to finish)
Len 4: 'chef' in 7 guesses (6 wrong: aiorln, 6 to finish)
Len 4: 'zaya' in 7 guesses (6 wrong: mrgnpv, 7 to finish)
Len 5: 'füdle' in 9 guesses (6 wrong: aiuoth, 6 to finish)
Len 5: 'wippe' in 8 guesses (6 wrong: anlktr, 8 to finish)
Len 5: 'ziege' in 9 guesses (6 wrong: smflrw, 6 to finish)
Len 5: 'haxen' in 10 guesses (6 wrong: rlmgbf, 7 to finish)
Len 5: 'debüt' in 8 guesses (6 wrong: aiourh, 6 to finish)
Len 6: 'tauber' in 9 guesses (6 wrong: lnskgp, 6 to finish)
Len 6: 'medium' in 7 guesses (6 wrong: nrlstg, 6 to finish)
Len 6: 'fasson' in 9 guesses (6 wrong: eirkmb, 6 to finish)
Len 6: 'schulz' in 11 guesses (6 wrong: eaiotd, 7 to finish)
Len 6: 'zörbig' in 7 guesses (6 wrong: eansou, 6 to finish)
Len 7: 'dämlack' in 7 guesses (6 wrong: eonhru, 7 to finish)
Len 7: 'curling' in 10 guesses (6 wrong: eathbm, 6 to finish)
Len 7: 'bexbach' in 7 guesses (6 wrong: nrslti, 6 to finish)
Len 7: 'fußfall' in 8 guesses (6 wrong: eonhrb, 6 to finish)
Len 7: 'gezücht' in 11 guesses (6 wrong: nrslim, 6 to finish)
Len 8: 'luftkuss' in 7 guesses (6 wrong: eaoicn, 6 to finish)
```
*Note that the first guess can be different depending on lengths, because each len has a unique letter distribution*

These words are pretty unsurprising. Due to the lower letter count, we have much less
information that narrows down the candidate count. There also is a lower
probability the letter appears in the guessed word, just because it is shorter.
Thus, we can generally expect worse performance on shorter words.
<figure class="plain">
	<img src="/img/hangman/letterCounting.png" alt="A chart, showing different strategies" />
	<figcaption> Average number of wrong guesses for every word length. Errors can be neglected with length > 20.</figcaption>
</figure>

### Letter distributions
It's actually pretty interesting, how the letter distribution changes after applying the new strategy:

<figure class="plain">
	<img src="/img/hangman/newDistrib.png" alt="Letter distribution on all words vs with the new Strategy" />
	<figcaption> Letter distribution on All words in the dataset vs just the ones Letter Counting lost on.</figcaption>
</figure>

We see, that unpopular letters such as *j, ß, x* or *q* much more popular in *difficult* words, confirming my theory. By looking at the outliers, we can conclude, that the choosing player should tendentially choose words with *a, u, k and specifically p and z* more, and *n,r and s* less often.

## Conclusion
<figure class="plain">
	<img src="/img/hangman/strategies.png" alt="A chart, showing different strategies" />
	<figcaption> The full distribution of guesses / word length</figcaption>
</figure>

We can see, that for long words, the game is pretty much trivial. To ensure a fair (and fun) game assuming perfect play, I suggest a system of dynamic lives according to this table:

<div class="table-scroll">
	<table>
		<tbody>
			<tr>
				<th scope="row">Word Length</th>
				<td>3</td>
				<td>4</td>
				<td>5</td>
				<td>6</td>
				<td>7/8</td>
				<td>9+</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<th scope="row">Lives</th> <td>6</td>
				<td>5</td>
				<td>4</td>
				<td>3</td>
				<td>2</td>
				<td>1</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<th scope="row">Hangman</th>
				<td class="cell-fig"><img src="/img/hangman/hangman-6.svg" alt="" /></td>
				<td class="cell-fig"><img src="/img/hangman/hangman-5.svg" alt="" /></td>
				<td class="cell-fig"><img src="/img/hangman/hangman-4.svg" alt="" /></td>
				<td class="cell-fig"><img src="/img/hangman/hangman-3.svg" alt="" /></td>
				<td class="cell-fig"><img src="/img/hangman/hangman-2.svg" alt="" /></td>
				<td class="cell-fig"><img src="/img/hangman/hangman-1.svg" alt="" /></td>
			</tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
</div>

Every colour in those hangmen is one life. As the count drops, parts get merged into a single stroke: both legs at once, then both arms, until for 9+ letters we just draw a head. 
*Hangmen drawn in excalidraw btw*

>Seriously try playing it this way! It actually is really fun. Thanks for reading this post, it took a lot of effort. Have a great day :)